      "completion_tokens": 1198,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical substance with the chemical formula **$H_2O$**. This means that each molecule of water is made up of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what makes water unique and important:\n\n### 1. Chemical Structure\n*   **Composition:** It consists of two parts hydrogen and one part oxygen.\n*   **Polarity:** The oxygen atom is more \"thirsty\" for electrons than the hydrogen atoms. This gives water a slight negative charge on one side and a slight positive charge on the other. This polarity is what allows water to stick to itself (surface tension) and to other substances.\n\n### 2. The Three States of Matter\nWater is unique because it is the only common substance on Earth that naturally exists in all three physical states:\n*   **Solid:** As ice (which floats on liquid water).\n*   **Liquid:** The state we use for drinking, washing, and swimming.\n*   **Gas:** As water vapor or steam (which rises into the atmosphere).\n\n### 3. Unique Physical Properties\nWater has several properties that make it essential for life:\n*   **Universal Solvent:** Water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This allows it to carry nutrients and minerals through the body and transport chemicals in the environment.\n*   **High Surface Tension:** The molecules at the surface of water stick together tightly, creating a \"skin.\" This allows small insects to walk across the surface of a pond without sinking.\n*   **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before it gets hot, and it releases a lot of heat before it freezes. This helps regulate the Earth's climate and keeps the temperature of the human body stable.\n\n### 4. Abundance\nWater is the most abundant molecule on the surface of the Earth. It covers about 71% of the planet and makes up about 60% of the human body. It is found in oceans, lakes, rivers, clouds, and underground aquifers."
